Subject: [PATCH] feat(studio): STUDIO-01 - the room visualiser gets the
 photographs too
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

CAM-02 made the renderer able to carry a photograph. /design started using it.
The room visualiser — the feature a person points at their own living room —
still did not: compositeFloor was called with no `grain`, so /floor-studio has
been drawing the same two-pigment floor it always drew while the page next door
draws oak. One product, two answers, which is the thing the shared renderer
exists to prevent.

It costs three times a drawn composite. Measured at studio size, 960x640:

    drawn          75ms
    photographic  212ms
    buildFloorMask 62ms   (already cached across configuration changes)

A fifth of a second spent in one call is a fifth of a second in which the page
ignores every click, and on a phone it is closer to a second. So compositeFloor
is now resumable in the same way renderFloorPlate is: `into` a buffer, `rows` a
band at a time. The studio renders the drawn floor synchronously as before — no
regression, on screen before the next paint, and what a person gets if the tile
never arrives — then fills in the photographic one a band per animation frame
and swaps it in WHOLE. Never half: a floor that is oak at the top and drawn at
the bottom has a seam across it that reads as a defect in the room rather than
as progress.

TWO THINGS THE BANDING HAD TO GET RIGHT, BOTH TESTED

The buffer starts as a COPY of the photograph, not as a blank. The rows a band
has not reached yet have to still be the room; a blank one is a hole in
somebody's living room. That is the only way to use this wrong, which is why
there is a createCompositeBuffer rather than a note saying to copy it yourself.

And `painted` — the fraction of the quad actually covered, which is what
decides whether a visitor is told their photograph is mostly furniture — is now
reported over the rows THIS call was asked for, alongside a new `considered`
count. The top band of a room is mostly wall and would drag an unweighted
average down on its own. Weighted by `considered` the banded figure comes out
equal to the whole-frame one to ten decimal places, on a room with a rug in it
so the number is not trivially 1.

Byte-identical to a single pass across all four patterns, with and without a
photograph, with a shared mask and without.

THE LIVE CAMERA VIEW STAYS DRAWN, AND THAT IS A DECISION

LiveRoom composites inside a requestAnimationFrame tick against an adaptive
resolution ladder. Tripling the per-frame cost there would not drop frames — the
ladder does not let it — it would pay for the photograph by dropping RESOLUTION,
by roughly a factor of three. A live camera view is being judged on whether the
floor tracks the room, and a sharp drawn floor that holds still against the
skirting board answers that question better than a soft photographic one at a
third of the size, where the grain is the first thing the downscale takes
anyway. The reasoning is written into LiveRoom.tsx so the next person to look
at it does not have to rediscover the measurement, along with what to change if
it is ever revisited: not a flag, a smaller tile and a fresh ladder reading.

65 of 66 guards. parse-scan clean. verify:css cannot run without an install.

